\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{How to read these notes}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The notes begin with one planar model neuron. Its state is a point
of the plane, and a voltage trace is the membrane-potential coordinate
of that point. From [State variables and phase space](fnip-0002)
onward, that model is the Morris–Lecar pair, and Part 1 stays with the
geometry needed for the later chapters.}

\p{Volume One is Parts 1 through 7, and the chapter numbers continue
through Volume Two. Part 1, Foundations of dynamical systems, is the
state, the trajectory, the equilibrium, and the periodic orbit in the
plane.
[State variables and phase space](fnip-0002) is its first chapter.
Part 2, Single-neuron dynamics, is the conductance-based models, the
classes of excitability, and irregular firing. Part 3, Synapses and
networks, is coupling, synchrony, and activity that continues between
stimuli. Part 4, Higher function, is motor control and perception as
the dynamics of a network. Part 5, Foundations of control theory, is
measurement, state, behavior, and intervention, the brain as a
controller, and the brain as an object of stimulation. Part 6,
Structure, is the membrane, the channels, and the cables that the
equations describe. Part 7, Development, is the growth that produces
that structure and those circuits. Volume Two is Parts 8 through 11. It reads the same phenomena as
algorithms: codes, memory, and action.
The root transcludes the chapters in one linear order, and that order
is the reading order.}

\p{Each later chapter has the same shape. It states a mathematical
tool, recovers a method familiar in neuroscience from that tool, keeps
a remark relating the two, and works an example. A reader who has met
the state and the trajectory in
[State variables and phase space](fnip-0002) has the selected
preparation for the next chapter of Part 1.}

\p{The reading-guide diagrams use an arrow for a selected preparation,
the preparation a reader of the later box needs from the earlier box,
and a question on an arrow for a question that reader would ask at the
turn. The overview diagram begins from the purpose of the notes and
runs through the parts. The Part 1 diagram begins from that purpose,
runs through [State variables and phase space](fnip-0002) and
[[fnip-0026]], and ends at a dotted box
for Part 2. A dotted box names a neighboring part. An arrow that
changes the subject carries one short question, in words already met.}

